if you're on a quest for some fantasy  netflix has you covered what's your  imagination itching for monster hunters  an evil witch a talking sheep keep  watching to find some films that have  all that and more  big fish is a movie about edward bloom a  man who's larger than life stories  turned out to be only partially  exaggerated when the film begins the  elderly bloom is dying from cancer and  so he retells his stories in flashback  form ewan mcgregor portrays a young  bloom as he befriends giants discovers a  hidden village deep in the swamp and is  attacked by a werewolf or working for a  circus bloom's son will has grown up  with these tall tales and has become  disillusioned with them but when he  starts to investigate his father's  claims he learns that there is a degree  of truth to all of them in the end it  becomes clear that bloom has lived a  spectacular life and the truth or  falsehood of his stories are  insignificant in comparison to the  immortalizing power of the stories  themselves big fish is a strange  beautiful film brought to life by  burton's signature off-beat style and  several fantastic performances most  notably that of mcgregor it also  features an academy award-nominated  original score if you haven't seen it it  should be first on your netflix list and  if you have there's never a bad time for  a revisit  trust me a man  if you'd like your fantasy films to also  be tear jerkers feast those brimming  eyes on a monster calls adapted by  screenwriter patrick ness from his own  2011 novel young connor o'malley's  mother is battling a life-threatening  illness leading him into conflict with  his strict grandmother played by  sigourney weaver and into an encounter  with the titular monster it's a massive  anthropomorphic yew tree voiced by liam  neeson in classic fairytale fashion the  monster insists on telling conor three  stories before demanding a story from  him in return as elements of the  monster's stories begin manifesting in  connor's mundane life the boy seeks the  monster's assistance in curing his  mother's disease  however the monster has a different  purpose in mind a different truth to be  drawn from the foreboding inevitable  fourth story gorgeously rendered and  even more gorgeously written a monster  calls as a modern fable about how we  deal with grief and the pain of lust and  a must watch for both drama lovers and  fantasy fans  if you like the idea of a fantasy story  with roots in ancient folklore but want  something with some whimsy turn your  netflix page to night books a film based  on the book by j.a white it is in turn a  modern kid-friendly retelling of the  middle eastern classic 1001 nights alex  moshe is a boy who loves writing scary  stories a habit that leads him into the  clutches of the witch natasha played by  kristen ritter who demands a scary story  be read to her every night trapped in  natasha's magical apartment alex must  use his imagination to keep himself  alive each day while figuring out a way  to escape but of course not everything  in the apartment is as it seems and as  alex grows closer to unveiling natasha's  secrets he must also face the ultimate  scary story the one he tells himself  did you say  scary stories  american author ailee martinez has seen  several of his books optioned over the  years but so far only one has actually  made it to the big screen that sole  exception is monster published in 2009 a  book about a modern day monster hunter  and the regular person who stumbles into  his life while the pandemic plague  chinese production monster run keeps his  basic premise very little else about the  film is recognizable to those who have  read the book that said it's still a  story worth watching cremo has long been  considered crazy for saying she can see  monsters but when she discovers a hidden  world in which monsters actually do  exist it turns out she's destined to  become more than she ever thought  possible the uniquely compelling visual  effects and imaginative world building  are what make this film stand out what  other movie could have both a  brilliantly executed time loop sequence  and the wisecracking sidekick character  who is literally a piece of paper  another well-known foreign film that  remains largely obscure to western  audiences is the mirrorboy a nigerian  film which was nominated for three  africa movie academy awards one of those  nominations went to edward cargatusi for  his portrayal of twelve-year-old tijon a  boy who grew up in london but is brought  back to his native gambia by his mother  after he gets into a fight no sooner  does tijan reach gambia however then he  is drawn away by the strange and  occasionally incorporal mirror boy this  figure takes him on a journey that will  see him reunited with his ancestors and  learn the truth about his mysterious  father while the question of whether or  not tijuan's odyssey and family history  are truly mystical in nature lies at the  heart of the film much of the plot also  involves a mother's search for her  missing son it answers that question  definitively as the story unfolds with a  magical meeting the mundane in a climax  flavoured with dark sorcery an evil  queen and the true nature of the mirror  boy himself  this list has recommended many different  kinds of fantasy but there's nothing  else quite like cosmo's laundromat an  animated short film created by the dutch  blender foundation it's the story of a  suicidal sheep named frank who  encounters possible salvation in form of  the enigmatic victor it's also an  absurdist masterpiece a beautiful and  bizarre creation that has no interest in  explaining itself and every interest in  leaving its audience in baffled  delighted or to explain any details of  the plot would do cosmos laundromat a  disservice it's a journey that captures  the true essence of fantasy  it's strange and wonderful infused with  the kind of magic that defies the limits  of reality and makes you believe  anything is possible even for a ragged  sheep who has given up hope anyone who  values the intangible truths of fantasy  storytelling should watch this film and  if you have netflix there's no excuse  not to it's only 10 minutes long your  time spent will be worth it  precious time  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite fantasy films are coming  soon subscribe to our youtube channel  and hit the bell so you don't miss a  single one  [Music]
 

